      "sectionBreaks": [
        {
          "before": "The large stone in Figure 96 illustrates a different case: an obstacle larger than the wavelength, especially much larger, can leave a region of weaker wave motion behind it, called a wave shadow.\nTreating that region as completely wave-free is an approximation; diffraction around the edges can still carry weaker waves into it.",
          "after": "Sound can reach places hidden behind an obstacle: you may hear a car's horn before you can see the car around a building corner.\nIn a wooded area, a call may reach a companion whose view is blocked by a tree trunk.\nBending around an obstacle does not mean that the sound must pass through the wall or the trunk."
        },
        {
          "before": "**Diffraction is the spreading of waves away from straight-line propagation near an obstacle's edge or an opening.**\nLike interference, diffraction is a property that wave processes can exhibit; water waves, sound, and light can all diffract.",
          "after": "Place a barrier with a slit narrower than the wavelength in the path of water waves to observe diffraction, as in Figure 97.\nBeyond the narrow slit, the wavefronts spread out as approximately circular arcs, as though a vibrating source were near the opening.\nThey do not simply continue as the incoming straight wavefronts."
        },
        {
          "before": "When the slit is wider than the wavelength, especially much wider, the waves beyond the barrier differ from the narrow-slit case, as in Figure 98.\nThe central parts of the wavefronts remain nearly straight, while the wavefronts bend near the edges.\nThis edge diffraction lets waves partly enter the region behind the barrier that straight paths would not reach; the whole wide opening does not behave like a point source.",
          "after": "Huygens' principle treats points on a known wavefront as secondary wave sources, helping explain why waves can bend around an obstacle's edge.\nThe geometrical envelope of those secondary waves alone does not explain every detail of a diffraction pattern, such as the strengths of the waves at different positions."
        }
      ],
